#%PAM-1.0
# Screen unlocking does not create a second PAM/logind session.
#
# This shares the distribution stack, including any pam_faillock tally: on
# stacks that count failures, a burst of failed unlocks can briefly contribute
# to that user's console/SSH counter. Counting is deliberately NOT redirected
# to a private tally:
#   * pam_faillock runs here as the unprivileged locker, and it treats an
#     unreadable tally as "no failures" while ignoring a failed authfail
#     write. A root-owned 0700 directory would therefore silently disable the
#     counter instead of protecting anything.
#   * A fixed directory writable by every user would let any local account
#     pre-create or corrupt another user's tally, turning the lockout into a
#     DoS. A per-user XDG_RUNTIME_DIR path cannot be expressed in a static
#     system-wide PAM policy.
# rix-lock's own in-process backoff (growing delays, 30 attempts per lock
# session, persisted across restarts under XDG_RUNTIME_DIR) is the
# authoritative limit here.
auth       include system-auth
account    include system-auth
